  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Which of the following are diagonal matrices?\n\n$$\nS=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "- $S$ is a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. Also, all the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are zeros.\n\nNotice that $S$ is not an identity matrix since the diagonal entries are not all ones.\n\n- $R$ is *not* a diagonal matrix since it's not a square matrix.\n\n- $Y$ is *not* a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. But some of the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are not zeros (for instance, ${y}_{31}=1\\ne 0$).\n\nTherefore, only $S$ is a diagonal matrix.",
